Title: "Generalized Unions and Intersections"
Id: "b05534b6-75b0-41df-be1f-7cef73f2e4a8"


Text: "Binary set operations extend naturally to families containing arbitrarily
       many—even uncountably many—sets. An arbitrary family \mathcal{F} of sets
       has a union \bigcup \mathcal{F} consisting of all elements belonging to
       at least one member set, and an intersection \bigcap \mathcal{F}
       consisting of all elements belonging to every member set."
Id: "7da339e7-8283-4946-93c7-19450a6e6268"


[\arbitrary.union{F}]
Defines: U is \set
when: F is \set
Documented:
. called: "arbitrary union $\bigcup \mathcal{F}$"
. written: "\bigcup \mathcal{F}?"
. description: "The set of all elements belonging to at least one set in family
                \mathcal{F}."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{24}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{34}
Id: "f3297b03-47a5-431a-9d4b-a9d7375b282a"


[\arbitrary.intersection{F}]
Defines: I is \set
when: F is \set
Documented:
. called: "arbitrary intersection $\bigcap \mathcal{F}$"
. written: "\bigcap \mathcal{F}?"
. description: "The set of all elements belonging to every set in non-empty
                family \mathcal{F}."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{25}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{35}
Id: "c805b714-c081-4c94-a73f-d70e2ae8bf00"


Text: "De Morgan's Laws generalize directly to arbitrary families: the
       complement of an arbitrary union is the arbitrary intersection of the
       individual complements, and the complement of an arbitrary intersection
       is the arbitrary union of the individual complements."
Id: "eec75449-f88f-4358-a0b2-6e025a93783c"


[\generalized.de.morgan.union]
Theorem:
given: F, U is \set
then:
. (\absolute.complement:of{\arbitrary.union{F}}:in{U} is \set)
Documented:
. called: "Generalized De Morgan's Law for Unions"
. written: "\left(\bigcup_{i \in I} A_i\right)^c = \bigcap_{i \in I} A_i^c"
. description: "The complement of an arbitrary union equals the intersection of
                the individual complements."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{26}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{36}
Id: "ebc660ab-8c37-4112-b7d4-4dcefb676c2f"


Text: "A family of sets \mathcal{F} is pairwise disjoint (or mutually disjoint)
       if no two distinct member sets share any elements in common: for any A, B
       \in \mathcal{F} with A \neq B, their intersection is empty."
Id: "956ed2ec-48f3-4982-b6db-5047e875e6ec"


[\pairwise.disjoint{F}]
States:
when: F is \set
that:
. forAll: A, B is \set
  where:
  . A "in"? F
  . B "in"? F
  . A != B
  then: ((A \.cap./ B) = \empty.set)
Documented:
. called: "pairwise disjoint family"
. written: "\operatorname{Disjoint}(\mathcal{F}?)"
. description: "The condition asserting that every pair of distinct sets in
                family \mathcal{F} has empty intersection."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{27}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{15}
Id: "6422e3d8-dccd-4676-a36a-3934f41d1467"


Text: "A partition of a set X is a collection \mathcal{P} of non-empty, pairwise
       disjoint subsets of X whose union equals X. Partitions represent the
       canonical set-theoretic decomposition of a universe into non-overlapping
       cells, providing the geometric foundation for equivalence relations and
       quotient structures."
Id: "01a98d1a-1109-4597-90fc-0b0544159f76"


[\set.partition:of{X}]
Declares: P is \set
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: A "in" P
  then: (A != \empty.set)
. \pairwise.disjoint{P}
. \arbitrary.union{P} = X
Documented:
. called: "partition of set $X?$"
. written: "\operatorname{Partition}(X?)"
. description: "A family of non-empty, pairwise disjoint subsets of set X whose
                union equals X."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{27}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{28}
Id: "2eb22011-bc22-4bed-b28e-ea7bd6184346"


Theorem:
given:
. X is \set
. P is \set.partition:of{X}
then:
. forAll: x "in" X
  then: (x "in"? X)
Documented:
. called: "exhaustive covering by partition"
. written: "\forall x \in X, \exists! A \in \mathcal{P}, x \in A"
. description: "Every element of set X belongs to exactly one block of partition
                \mathcal{P}."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{28}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{29}
Id: "45f1faaf-356a-4b13-9123-2753b1787e04"
